61w|a|A bucket or scoop used in bailing water out of a boat. ; To deliver; to release. ; To set free, or deliver from arrest, or out of custody, on the undertaking of some other person or persons that he or they will be responsible for the appearance, at a certain day and place, of the person bailed. ; To deliver, as goods in trust, for some special object or purpose, upon a contract, expressed or implied, that the trust shall be faithfully executed on the part of the bailee, or person intrusted; as, to bail cloth to a tailor to be made into a garment; to bail goods to a carrier. ; Custody; keeping. ; The person or persons who procure the release of a prisoner from the custody of the officer, or from imprisonment, by becoming surely for his appearance in court. ; The security given for the appearance of a prisoner in order to obtain his release from custody of the officer; as, the man is out on bail; to go bail for any one. ; The arched handle of a kettle, pail, or similar vessel, usually movable. ; A half hoop for supporting the cover of a carrier's wagon, awning of a boat, etc. ; A line of palisades serving as an exterior defense. ; The outer wall of a feudal castle. Hence: The space inclosed by it; the outer court. ; A certain limit within a forest. ; A division for the stalls of an open stable. ; The top or cross piece ( or either of the two cross pieces) of the wicket.	e:mtw,10,1~mtw,10,1
